Routing Fundamentals ka matlab hota hai ki jab koi client (jaise browser ya curl) kisi HTTP method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) aur URL path (/, /about, /users/{id}) ke sath request bhejta hai, toh Axum server ko kaise pata chale ki kis function ko execute karna hai.

Axum me routing bohot hi clean, type-safe aur macro-free hoti hai. Aaiye iske saare core concepts ko ek fully runnable example ke sath dekhte hain, jisme basic routes, method chaining, path parameters ({id} syntax), aur nested routers sabhi shamil hain.
Step 1: Cargo.toml

Apne project ki Cargo.toml file me yeh dependencies rakhein:
Ini, TOML

[package]
name = "axum_routing_demo"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[dependencies]
axum = "0.8"
tokio = { version = "1.0", features = ["full"] }

Step 2: Complete src/main.rs Code

Yeh code routing ke saare main concepts (Basic routes, Method Chaining, Path Parameters, aur Nested Routers) ko cover karta hai:
Rust

use axum::{
    extract::Path,
    routing::{get, post},
    Router,
};

// 1. Basic Handlers
async fn home_handler() -> &'static str {
    "Welcome to Home Page (GET /)"
}

async fn about_handler() -> &'static str {
    "This is the About Page (GET /about)"
}

// 2. Handlers for Chained Methods (/users)
async fn list_users_handler() -> &'static str {
    "List of all users (GET /users)"
}

async fn create_user_handler() -> &'static str {
    "User created successfully (POST /users)"
}

// 3. Path Parameter Handler (Axum 0.8 syntax: {id})
async fn get_user_by_id_handler(Path(id): Path<u32>) -> String {
    format!("Fetching user profile for ID: {} (GET /users/{{id}})", id)
}

// 4. Nested Router Handler (/api/v1/status)
async fn api_status_handler() -> &'static str {
    "API v1 is running smoothly (GET /api/v1/status)"
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    // --- A. Sub-router for API nesting ---
    let v1_router = Router::new()
        .route("/status", get(api_status_handler));

    // --- B. Main Router with Routing Fundamentals ---
    let app = Router::new()
        // 1. Basic Routes
        .route("/", get(home_handler))
        .route("/about", get(about_handler))
        
        // 2. Multiple Methods on One Route (Chaining)
        .route("/users", get(list_users_handler).post(create_user_handler))
        
        // 3. Path Parameters (Axum 0.8 style: {id} instead of :id)
        .route("/users/{id}", get(get_user_by_id_handler))
        
        // 4. Nested Routers using .nest()
        .nest("/api/v1", v1_router);

    // Bind and start the server
    let listener = tokio::net::TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:3000").await.unwrap();
    println!("Server started successfully at http://127.0.0.1:3000");

    axum::serve(listener, app).await.unwrap();
}

Step 3: Run aur Test Kaise Karein?

Terminal me run karein:
Bash

cargo run

Ab dusre terminal me alag-alag routes ko test karne ke liye ye commands chalaayein:

    Basic GET Route:
    Bash

    curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/
    # Output: Welcome to Home Page (GET /)

    Method Chaining (GET /users):
    Bash

    curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/users
    # Output: List of all users (GET /users)

    Method Chaining (POST /users):
    Bash

    curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/users
    # Output: User created successfully (POST /users)

    Path Parameter (/users/{id}) - Axum 0.8 Syntax:
    Bash

    curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/users/99
    # Output: Fetching user profile for ID: 99 (GET /users/{id})

    (Note: Axum 0.8 me : colon syntax jaise /users/:id allowed nahi hai aur server start hote hi panic/crash kar jayega. Hamesha {id} format use karna hota hai).

    Nested Router (/api/v1/status):
    Bash

    curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/v1/status
    # Output: API v1 is running smoothly (GET /api/v1/status)